{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issues_index.json\\\")); nums={4043,3492,3781,213,247}; [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in d if x.get(\\\"number\\\") in nums]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"number\": 213, \"title\": \"Discord.test\", \"created_at\": \"2020-08-05T22:33:54Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-08-05T22:34:35Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"\"}\n{\"number\": 247, \"title\": \"[QUESTION] How to test output of rich.Table?\", \"created_at\": \"2020-08-22T19:59:57Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-08-23T13:50:06Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Hey, first thanks for this awesome package.\\r\\n\\r\\nI have a small question on how to test for output of a rich `Table`, assume I have the following code:\\r\\n```\\r\\ntable = Table(title=f'[bold]MyTitle of {myproject}', =\\\"blue\\\", header_style=Style(color=\\\"blue\\\", bold=True), box=HEAVY_HEAD)\\r\\n\\r\\n        table.add_column(\\\"Column1\\\", justify=\\\"left\\\", style=\\\"green\\\", no_wrap=True)\\r\\n        table.add_column(\\\"Column2\\\", justify=\\\"left\\\")\\r\\n        table.add_column(\\\"Column3\\\", justify=\\\"left\\\")\\r\\n\\r\\n        for item in mylist:\\r\\n            table.add_row(f'[bold]{template[0]}', template[1], template[2], template[3], template[4])\\r\\n\\r\\n        console = Console()\\r\\n        console.print(table)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nNow while testing this (with different values for `myproject`) using pytest I'd like to check if this table meets some requirements (so I'd like to check for example if the title is right).\\r\\n\\r\\nHow would one do this?\\r\\nMy naive approach was just to capture the output with pytest (basically a big string) a test whether it contains my desired title. However this does not work, as the output is something like:\\r\\n```\\r\\nMyTitle of title1                        \\\\n┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━... PyPi.     │           │         │\\\\n└────────────────────────────┴────────────┴──────────────┴───────────┴─────────┘\\\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nSo TL;DR: Is there any nice way to test the output of a table? Thanks in advance ;)\"}\n{\"number\": 3492, \"title\": \"[REQUEST] logging exceptions: highlight paths to my code\", \"created_at\": \"2024-09-15T05:17:58Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2024-11-01T16:12:23Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"In exceptions, can rich automatically highlight paths of my code, to be able to distinguish from code in the virtualenv or external packages. For example, in the following exception, the middle path should be highlighted.\\r\\n\\r\\n```py\\r\\n/Users/mustafa/Library/Caches/pypoetry/virtualenvs/foobar-Ra8Pwy-F-py3.12/lib/python3.12/site-packages/ninja/main.py:515\\r\\n/Users/mustafa/dev/project/api.py:38\\r\\n/Users/mustafa/Library/Caches/pypoetry/virtualenvs/foobar-Ra8Pwy-F-py3.12/lib/python3.12/site-packages/ninja/operation.py:121\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nBenefits\\r\\n\\r\\n- Faster error source identification: Instantly spot errors in your code vs. external libraries.\\r\\n- Improved debugging efficiency: Quickly determine if an issue is in your code or a third-party library.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\nedit: [claude suggested using excepthook](https://claude.site/artifacts/527c9d38-f29d-4c9d-b47c-4bf1833dd342). not sure if the solution makes sense. \"}\n{\"number\": 3781, \"title\": \"Test\", \"created_at\": \"2025-06-23T09:31:52Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2025-06-23T09:32:22Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Testing the auto responder\"}\n{\"number\": 4043, \"title\": \"test - will delete\", \"created_at\": \"2026-03-25T02:37:55Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2026-03-25T02:38:09Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"test\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
